#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562519} :activity/id #uuid "c59feace-40c5-5a98-a7eb-6db0b5b34eb0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050714 :topic/id #uuid "60b97f00-f86a-5a4f-834e-1a83f8ee3cc2" :topic/math-academy-id 1453} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2b084c-2030-569c-b3db-c1f0bb240497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108674} {:answer/id #uuid "a1178c63-4702-5bdc-9e41-c3046acb6f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108675} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dca092-57eb-5e07-8d0d-0d53376fcddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108676} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1a8afd-47ad-5ee5-aef2-253b8da8405f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108677} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b6b5a3-b768-5bb1-8d7e-9d7a7b3b6af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186108674} :answer-field/id #uuid "af420e31-aeb4-5e79-ae37-c4b53833e453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d39d8f8-c6eb-55ec-8766-ad9675623f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37821" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d51a8e78f8b8c1f05e502bd98aed6a253799336765f1331d23e63ec637bcb448.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/438ce908c902696b480bf9791cae089be40f9e7b2552208e13e08405496b696c.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0090e87d-d8ba-537b-bdb4-37a028de7d1c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dad645d-0415-5658-be31-35d722e7881e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561854} {:answer/id #uuid "b7802217-fcc7-5486-bbd8-0b67e0f11610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562042} {:answer/id #uuid "c86f1ffc-4ac3-5b6a-963c-de4bf912162e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562081} {:answer/id #uuid "e20fd379-fb1a-5dbe-a692-d26f272c9382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562139} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a827f2-e12d-53fa-8215-157177df99c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562081} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd1bfcef-f919-5cd5-b2a5-2c3687f0610d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b33de5dd-a44b-50a7-ac6a-5ef9965fa83e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65061" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e2/e2c83b965f7108bff3165531446cce43006a41b9742914e8fc40c50741230427.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "47f3a656-f0bc-5501-a979-06d1b6cd331d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffeba9c-70a6-5328-9dc3-5020e0313ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} {:answer/id #uuid "4eafe18b-8c02-5577-a960-94ba6f971ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333711} {:answer/id #uuid "7213fed3-dd70-59f5-9da6-eb714e406490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334021} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc0b081-37ac-56aa-8ceb-99b894130c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334379} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4516a-8d82-5f18-ae27-7aaf1618cf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0192dd7-c731-5b69-8d4c-3c9bab7026de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "555c1e17-369e-580d-8f06-901f476b3eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-65064" :question/problem "![](images/45/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/44/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a6cbd5b1-b78d-5262-a311-97b451bde36f"}] :activity/title "Using the Pythagorean Identity in the First Quadrant" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561575}]}
